1
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:07,600
OK, let's get started

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,800
So now, let's talk a bit about a recap of the online RL

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,040
methods that we've talked about, so far

4
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:18,400
So we started with talking about policy gradients, where

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,920
our goal was to collect data from our policy

6
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:25,440
then increase the likelihood on actions that

7
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:28,380
led to above average outcomes

8
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:29,880
So encouraging the policy to do more

9
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:31,540
of the stuff that led to good outcomes

10
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:33,920
less of the stuff that led to bad outcomes

11
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:39,780
And then we started introducing this notion of a value function

12
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:41,240
which can then estimate

13
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:44,640
We can use a value function to estimate how good an action is

14
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:47,100
and then when we fit a value function

15
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,040
we can use that to then encourage the policy

16
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:53,840
to do more of what you estimated to be good and less of what you

17
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:55,760
estimated to be bad

18
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:59,790
And we saw a couple algorithms, like PPO and SAC

19
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:04,150
that use this actor critic approach

20
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:08,670
We also talked about a few different ways for fitting value

21
00:01:08,670 --> 00:01:14,390
functions, and I want to quickly go through these different ways

22
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:15,910
again, because it's actually going

23
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:19,190
to play a pretty important role in today's lecture

24
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:21,470
So the first thing we talked about

25
00:01:21,470 --> 00:01:25,950
is, if you have some state in your data

26
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:28,270
and you want to estimate the value of that state

27
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:34,270
one way to do that is to take the sum of the rewards

28
00:01:34,270 --> 00:01:37,310
over the trajectory in the future

29
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:40,350
and estimate the value that way

30
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:47,670
So the target would be you'd be trying to fit some V pi of S

31
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:52,590
and your target would be the sum of rewards from that state

32
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:53,210
onward

33
00:01:53,210 --> 00:02:01,110
So you'd be trying to contrast this

34
00:02:01,110 --> 00:02:08,710
with the sum of rewards at future time steps--

35
00:02:08,710 --> 00:02:10,990
from future time steps, from t to t

36
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:15,510
And then your objective for your value function

37
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:18,310
would look something like this, where you're then

38
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:27,190
trying to minimize this over the states and actions in your data

39
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:30,510
set

40
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:31,950
So that was the first simplest way

41
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:34,630
that we saw to estimate a value function

42
00:02:34,630 --> 00:02:37,270
And then we saw a way to potentially get

43
00:02:37,270 --> 00:02:38,030
a better estimate

44
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:40,610
Because instead of just summing all the rewards

45
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:43,150
you could actually look at your estimate of the value

46
00:02:43,150 --> 00:02:45,190
at an intermediate state

47
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:50,070
So say that this is S prime is your next state right here

48
00:02:50,070 --> 00:02:53,170
Instead of looking at the sum of rewards from there

49
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:56,140
instead you could look at what is the reward at the next time

50
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:57,520
step plus the value--

51
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:59,820
my estimate for the value at the next time step

52
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:01,740
And use that as your target, where

53
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:03,860
instead of being the sum of rewards

54
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:10,460
the target would be the reward at the current time step

55
00:03:10,460 --> 00:03:21,100
plus the estimated value at the next time step

56
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:26,940
And this is alternative target for fitting a value function

57
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:29,610
And so the full objective would be--

58
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:34,420
whoops

59
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:39,460
The full objective would be something like this

60
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:49,440
And we're trying to minimize this over our data set

61
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:53,940
We could say our value function has parameters, phi

62
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:57,380
We called this bootstrapping or TD updates, temporal difference

63
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:59,240
updates

64
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:01,780
So that was the second way that we saw for estimating a value

65
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:03,100
function

66
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:05,220
And then we also saw a third way

67
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:11,380
So in these two scenarios, the data set

68
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:13,800
is sampled from your policy

69
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,540
These were on policy methods for estimating

70
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:18,860
the value of a policy

71
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:21,860
So these were scenarios, where your data

72
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:28,060
set was sampled from the policy that you're trying to evaluate

73
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:32,260
And we then started to look at a scenario, where what

74
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:40,380
if your data set is sampled from some mixture of old policies

75
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:45,020
Could we use that data in order to fit a value function

76
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:55,610
And to do that, we started looking at Q-functions

77
00:04:55,610 --> 00:04:57,650
And with Q-functions, we can again use something

78
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:00,290
like bootstrapping, where in principle, we

79
00:05:00,290 --> 00:05:06,890
could use something like Q hat pi of phi of s t, a t

80
00:05:06,890 --> 00:05:08,610
we're trying to estimate this

81
00:05:08,610 --> 00:05:13,370
And then we could also use this as our target

82
00:05:13,370 --> 00:05:15,370
with the caveat that this value function is

83
00:05:15,370 --> 00:05:18,410
going to be for the policy and the data

84
00:05:18,410 --> 00:05:23,870
and not for the policy that we're trying to learn

85
00:05:23,870 --> 00:05:26,570
and the policy that we're trying to learn a value function for

86
00:05:26,570 --> 00:05:31,730
And so what we did instead is we changed our target

87
00:05:31,730 --> 00:05:35,490
to use the Q-function

88
00:05:35,490 --> 00:05:45,730
So we had the target as s t plus 1 a t plus 1

89
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:51,030
And the key difference here and s and a

90
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:52,930
and s t plus 1 are sampled fro the data

91
00:05:52,930 --> 00:05:53,650
Set

92
00:05:53,650 --> 00:05:58,770
But a t plus 1 is going to be sampled from the policy

93
00:05:58,770 --> 00:06:09,450
that we're trying to learn a value function for

94
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:10,230
Cool

95
00:06:10,230 --> 00:06:12,770
So that's a quick recap of some of the things

96
00:06:12,770 --> 00:06:14,970
that we talked about

97
00:06:14,970 --> 00:06:18,410
And these are the different ways of estimating

98
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:21,650
value functions, with the key difference of this last one

99
00:06:21,650 --> 00:06:27,250
being something that we can use data from old policies for

100
00:06:27,250 --> 00:06:32,210
Now, one really important aspect of this last one

101
00:06:32,210 --> 00:06:35,570
is that because we're bootstrapping and using

102
00:06:35,570 --> 00:06:38,090
our own estimate of the value function

103
00:06:38,090 --> 00:06:41,210
at a given state as the label, we

104
00:06:41,210 --> 00:06:48,040
need this Q-function to be trained on actions that actually

105
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:49,760
would resemble the actions that we're

106
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:51,580
going to be evaluating it on

107
00:06:51,580 --> 00:06:54,000
And so if our policy is very different from our set

108
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,400
of old policies, this label will not be accurate

109
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:02,280
And then we won't be able to learn an accurate Q-function

110
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:05,480
So it needs good coverage over action

111
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:06,640
Cool

112
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:08,880
And then those are the different ways

113
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:10,180
of learning value functions

114
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:13,480
The full off-policy actor-critic method

115
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:18,820
looks something like this, where we took data from our policy

116
00:07:18,820 --> 00:07:21,520
stored that in the buffer that had a number of data

117
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:23,280
from a number of past policies

118
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:25,960
Sampled some data from that buffer

119
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:29,120
updated our Q-function with these targets

120
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:31,680
and then updated our policy to encourage

121
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:36,560
it to increase the likelihood on actions that had a high Q-value

122
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:39,000
And then we can repeat that process

123
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,380
So for today, we're going to be building on some of those ideas

124
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:44,840
And specifically, we're going to be

125
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:47,200
talking about offline reinforcement learning

126
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:50,660
And in offline reinforcement learning

127
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:56,200
we're trying to learn from a data set that was given to us

128
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:58,480
not collected by our own policy

129
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:00,440
And we'll then talk about two different methods

130
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:02,120
or two different classes of methods

131
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:04,520
for doing offline reinforcement learning

132
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:07,200
And the key things that we'll try to see is--

133
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:09,200
I talked about is, first, the key challenge that

134
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:10,800
arises in offline RL

135
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:13,280
Second, two different approaches for offline

136
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:15,000
RL and why they work

137
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,080
And lastly, how offline reinforcement learning

138
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:19,480
can improve over just imitation learning, which

139
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:22,280
is another way to learn from offline data sets

140
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:23,780
The question is, is this Q-learning

141
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:26,200
This is actually two lectures ago

142
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:28,680
This is soft actor-critic

143
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:34,799
In Q-learning. , there is one last change to this

144
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:39,120
So in this case, we're trying to learn Q-function for the policy

145
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:40,640
that we're learning

146
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:43,320
The difference that we saw in Q-learning

147
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:45,550
was we were actually trying to learn the Q-function

148
00:08:45,550 --> 00:08:47,230
for the optimal policy

149
00:08:47,230 --> 00:08:58,670
And in Q-learning-- this red marker is not great--

150
00:08:58,670 --> 00:09:00,830
our target was instead--

151
00:09:00,830 --> 00:09:02,670
instead of sampling from the policy

152
00:09:02,670 --> 00:09:04,670
that we're trying to learn a value function for

153
00:09:04,670 --> 00:09:08,670
we tried to pick an action that was the best action according

154
00:09:08,670 --> 00:09:09,830
to that Q-function

155
00:09:09,830 --> 00:09:18,010
And so our target was of s t, a t, plus gamma

156
00:09:18,010 --> 00:09:23,750
And then we were taking a max over the next action

157
00:09:23,750 --> 00:09:30,350
so that we were actually trying to--

158
00:09:30,350 --> 00:09:35,470
and our goal here was to try to learn the Q-function

159
00:09:35,470 --> 00:09:38,210
for the optimal policy

160
00:09:38,210 --> 00:09:39,710
And this was also something that can

161
00:09:39,710 --> 00:09:44,170
be done off policy, without data from the optimal policy

162
00:09:44,170 --> 00:09:48,270
as long as your estimates have good coverage

163
00:09:48,270 --> 00:09:51,430
So in offline reinforcement learning--

164
00:09:51,430 --> 00:09:53,450
first, why offline reinforcement learning

165
00:09:53,450 --> 00:09:57,750
So the process for offline for online reinforcement learning

166
00:09:57,750 --> 00:09:58,810
is, you collect data

167
00:09:58,810 --> 00:10:00,910
Then, you update the policy, either

168
00:10:00,910 --> 00:10:04,590
using the latest data or the data, so far

169
00:10:04,590 --> 00:10:06,700
And then you repeat that process

170
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:08,870
With offline RL, you're actually just given the data

171
00:10:08,870 --> 00:10:11,190
set in advance

172
00:10:11,190 --> 00:10:13,030
And then you want to train a policy

173
00:10:13,030 --> 00:10:15,390
on that provided data set

174
00:10:15,390 --> 00:10:20,190
So you're not actually iteratively collecting data

175
00:10:20,190 --> 00:10:21,950
And there's a number of circumstances

176
00:10:21,950 --> 00:10:23,710
where offline reinforcement learning might

177
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:28,430
be more useful than online reinforcement learning

178
00:10:28,430 --> 00:10:31,730
The first is that when you're trying to approach a problem

179
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:33,350
you might already have a data set that

180
00:10:33,350 --> 00:10:36,950
was collected by an existing system or by people

181
00:10:36,950 --> 00:10:42,220
and collecting data from your current policy might be risky

182
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:43,940
or might be unsafe

183
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:46,100
So, for example, if you're trying

184
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:49,020
to learn a policy for controlling a car

185
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:51,540
to drive on roads, you might have

186
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:53,540
a lot of data of people driving, that

187
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:55,580
could be very useful for learning

188
00:10:55,580 --> 00:10:57,780
a good policy for driving

189
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:00,180
And it might be unsafe to actually try

190
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:03,260
to deploy your policy, especially a policy that

191
00:11:03,260 --> 00:11:07,100
is not very good at driving

192
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:09,160
In other circumstances, people have

193
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:10,740
looked at using reinforcement learning

194
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:14,620
for figuring out good ways to treat people

195
00:11:14,620 --> 00:11:16,220
in medical scenarios

196
00:11:16,220 --> 00:11:18,660
So, for example, there was a really cool project

197
00:11:18,660 --> 00:11:24,010
on a seizure prevention use case

198
00:11:24,010 --> 00:11:26,260
where you're trying to learn a policy for figuring out

199
00:11:26,260 --> 00:11:27,780
how to prevent seizures

200
00:11:27,780 --> 00:11:29,940
This is a scenario where if you have

201
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:33,180
an offline method of some existing system, then

202
00:11:33,180 --> 00:11:35,980
you could use that to learn a policy, without having

203
00:11:35,980 --> 00:11:38,700
to collect data from a policy that

204
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:42,660
is not good in the first place

205
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:44,500
And then the last point, which is actually

206
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:48,420
a little bit more nuanced, is that if you actually

207
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:51,380
run online or all multiple times

208
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:54,760
you might actually already have a data set from your policies

209
00:11:54,760 --> 00:11:56,260
And it would be really nice to reuse

210
00:11:56,260 --> 00:11:59,780
that data set rather than trying to recollect data

211
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:04,130
every single time you try to run an experiment

212
00:12:04,130 --> 00:12:06,500
If, for example, you're training a robot to do something

213
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:09,020
and you want it to learn how to grasp something

214
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:11,770
then if you have a pure online RL method

215
00:12:11,770 --> 00:12:14,400
it means that every single time you need to run your algorithm

216
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:17,860
you need to recollect data from your policy

217
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:20,900
And that can be pretty inefficient, because probably

218
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:23,540
the first time you run the algorithm isn't--

219
00:12:23,540 --> 00:12:27,540
you still room for iteration on the algorithm itself

220
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:31,740
And generally, online data is more expensive than offline data

221
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:36,570
for that reason, because offline data, you can reuse many times

222
00:12:36,570 --> 00:12:38,970
Also worth noting that you can blend offline

223
00:12:38,970 --> 00:12:41,430
RL and online RL, where you have some existing data set

224
00:12:41,430 --> 00:12:43,490
Like in autonomous driving, you have a data set

225
00:12:43,490 --> 00:12:47,150
of people driving, and then you want to train a policy on that

226
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:49,610
And then you try to improve it with some online data

227
00:12:49,610 --> 00:12:52,330
collection, perhaps with a safety driver

228
00:12:52,330 --> 00:12:54,890
after you have a policy that's pretty good

229
00:12:54,890 --> 00:12:58,690
And there's a number of real-world use cases, where

230
00:12:58,690 --> 00:13:00,190
you will want to blend it

231
00:13:00,190 --> 00:13:02,310
Because if you actually are deploying your policy

232
00:13:02,310 --> 00:13:04,210
then it's great to be able to improve it

233
00:13:04,210 --> 00:13:06,310
after you deploy your policy

234
00:13:06,310 --> 00:13:06,810
Yeah

235
00:13:06,810 --> 00:13:09,610
So the question is, can you view offline RL as a special case

236
00:13:09,610 --> 00:13:12,930
of online, but off-policy RL, but where

237
00:13:12,930 --> 00:13:14,170
you don't know the data

238
00:13:14,170 --> 00:13:18,920
And that's definitely one that's very--

239
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:21,970
that's a correct way to view it

240
00:13:21,970 --> 00:13:24,650
One thing that is a very strict requirement in offline RL

241
00:13:24,650 --> 00:13:27,210
in pure offline RL, is that you have no ability

242
00:13:27,210 --> 00:13:28,890
to collect more data

243
00:13:28,890 --> 00:13:30,480
And in that sense, it's not really

244
00:13:30,480 --> 00:13:31,650
a special case of online RL

245
00:13:31,650 --> 00:13:35,850
Because in online off-policy RL, you can collect more data

246
00:13:35,850 --> 00:13:38,690
That said, we'll see that some of the techniques

247
00:13:38,690 --> 00:13:42,130
will draw upon some of these off policy techniques

248
00:13:42,130 --> 00:13:43,850
from an algorithm standpoint

249
00:13:43,850 --> 00:13:47,090
Because in these scenarios, you're

250
00:13:47,090 --> 00:13:49,530
able to use data from old policies

251
00:13:49,530 --> 00:13:51,570
and that's very useful when you have

252
00:13:51,570 --> 00:13:54,610
an offline data set from some policy that you don't know

253
00:13:54,610 --> 00:13:58,590
So now let's talk about this a little bit more formally

254
00:13:58,590 --> 00:14:04,270
So say that you're given some data set

255
00:14:04,270 --> 00:14:12,990
and the data set, maybe, has some trajectories in it

256
00:14:12,990 --> 00:14:14,970
Maybe if it's a driving scenario

257
00:14:14,970 --> 00:14:22,370
then you have some car, and you have data sets of people driving

258
00:14:22,370 --> 00:14:24,990
in a number of different ways, in a number of different places

259
00:14:24,990 --> 00:14:27,010
with a number of different cars

260
00:14:27,010 --> 00:14:34,640
And we're going to say that this constitutes

261
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:37,280
some data set that we're given

262
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:43,320
And it's helpful to formally think about the policy that

263
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:44,700
collected this data

264
00:14:44,700 --> 00:14:46,780
Now, we don't know the underlying policy

265
00:14:46,780 --> 00:14:49,480
So for example, if this was collected by people

266
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:51,380
we don't know what--

267
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:53,420
well, first, it was collected by multiple people

268
00:14:53,420 --> 00:14:55,120
then it's multiple policies

269
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:57,920
And we also just don't directly have access to that

270
00:14:57,920 --> 00:15:00,200
But it's useful to think about that policy

271
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:02,460
just from a mathematical standpoint

272
00:15:02,460 --> 00:15:06,400
And so we'll say that this data set was collected

273
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:13,240
by running some policy that will refer to as the behavior policy

274
00:15:13,240 --> 00:15:16,800
or pi beta

275
00:15:16,800 --> 00:15:20,660
And if it's collected by multiple policies

276
00:15:20,660 --> 00:15:24,760
then you can think of pi beta as a mixture of policies

277
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:32,720
And you can define that mixture as some average or weighted sum

278
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:36,040
over some constituent policies

279
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:39,000
So we don't lose any generality by saying that it's

280
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:43,080
collected by a single policy

281
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:43,620
Cool

282
00:15:43,620 --> 00:15:48,960
So that's the data set sample from some unknown policy

283
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:51,920
Maybe a mixture of policies

284
00:15:51,920 --> 00:15:57,020
If we were to think about the distribution over this data

285
00:15:57,020 --> 00:15:59,160
we could think of it, having some underlying state

286
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:03,560
distribution, corresponding to this policy, an action

287
00:16:03,560 --> 00:16:08,280
distribution dynamics, a reward function

288
00:16:08,280 --> 00:16:15,280
And then our goal is to learn a good policy that

289
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:17,880
maximizes reward

290
00:16:17,880 --> 00:16:21,280
And so ideally, we would be able to take this data set

291
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:27,780
and be able to learn some policy that can control the car

292
00:16:27,780 --> 00:16:30,930
And ideally, it's maybe a pretty good policy

293
00:16:30,930 --> 00:16:34,110
Maybe some of the times, it does merge into another lane

294
00:16:34,110 --> 00:16:39,350
But generally, we want a policy that will refer to as pi theta

295
00:16:39,350 --> 00:16:45,070
And we want to be able to ultimately maximize

296
00:16:45,070 --> 00:16:50,110
an objective, corresponding to the sum of rewards for how good

297
00:16:50,110 --> 00:16:51,710
this policy is

298
00:16:51,710 --> 00:16:54,310
And so you can think about that as maximizing

299
00:16:54,310 --> 00:16:56,990
the parameters of this policy and trying

300
00:16:56,990 --> 00:17:04,650
to maximize for trajectories sampled from this policy

301
00:17:04,650 --> 00:17:11,230
maximizing the sum of rewards

302
00:17:11,230 --> 00:17:14,430
Or, well, either--

303
00:17:14,430 --> 00:17:16,369
I guess I'll write it as r of r of trajectory

304
00:17:16,369 --> 00:17:21,150
where this is the sum of rewards for individual states

305
00:17:21,150 --> 00:17:25,589
in that trajectory

306
00:17:25,589 --> 00:17:30,790
Now, one thing to note here is that this objective

307
00:17:30,790 --> 00:17:34,590
is an expectation over this policy

308
00:17:34,590 --> 00:17:39,190
Whereas, our data was collected by a different policy

309
00:17:39,190 --> 00:17:42,070
And so we can think about this as there being some distribution

310
00:17:42,070 --> 00:17:44,190
shift between the policy that collected

311
00:17:44,190 --> 00:17:46,870
the data and the policy that we're deploying

312
00:17:46,870 --> 00:17:49,990
And we don't actually get to sample from that policy

313
00:17:49,990 --> 00:17:52,310
And so that's one of the big challenges

314
00:17:52,310 --> 00:17:56,230
in offline reinforcement learning

315
00:17:56,230 --> 00:17:57,830
So there's this distribution shift

316
00:17:57,830 --> 00:18:00,750
because this expectation is under the learned policy

317
00:18:00,750 --> 00:18:04,150
rather than under the policy that collected the data

318
00:18:04,150 --> 00:18:06,590
And then it's worth mentioning that the dynamics

319
00:18:06,590 --> 00:18:08,230
of the environment are going to be

320
00:18:08,230 --> 00:18:12,850
constant between the data and the learned policy

321
00:18:12,850 --> 00:18:16,090
So the distribution shift is only for these trajectories

322
00:18:16,090 --> 00:18:20,150
It's only caused by the shift in the policy

323
00:18:20,150 --> 00:18:23,240
The question is, if the data set is comprehensive enough

324
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:24,820
can it turn out that the learned learn

325
00:18:24,820 --> 00:18:31,860
policy and the behavior policy distribution are identical

326
00:18:31,860 --> 00:18:35,310
Generally, that's extremely rare

327
00:18:35,310 --> 00:18:36,600
Generally, that doesn't happen

328
00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:43,940
And the reason is that, usually-- well, first, you

329
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:46,380
often want to improve over your data set

330
00:18:46,380 --> 00:18:50,140
and learn something that's better than the average driver

331
00:18:50,140 --> 00:18:52,240
And so in that case, you might actually

332
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:53,700
end up with a narrower distribution

333
00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:56,740
here than your original policy

334
00:18:56,740 --> 00:18:59,220
In the case where your original policy is very good

335
00:18:59,220 --> 00:19:02,680
then you're somewhat more in an imitation learning like setting

336
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:05,580
and you might still diverge and drift away

337
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:12,350
from the distribution in the data set

338
00:19:12,350 --> 00:19:13,600
Where does the data come from

339
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:16,260
It can be human collected from some hand-designed system

340
00:19:16,260 --> 00:19:18,180
or controller

341
00:19:18,180 --> 00:19:21,260
It could also be from previous reinforcement learning runs

342
00:19:21,260 --> 00:19:22,240
that you want to reuse

343
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:24,220
It could be a mixture of these sources

344
00:19:24,220 --> 00:19:29,820
Lots of different places the data could come from

345
00:19:29,820 --> 00:19:32,460
Now, there were some comments about how

346
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:35,300
this offline reinforcement learning relates to off policy

347
00:19:35,300 --> 00:19:36,620
reinforcement learning

348
00:19:36,620 --> 00:19:38,740
And one natural question is maybe

349
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:42,180
we could just reuse an off-policy algorithm

350
00:19:42,180 --> 00:19:46,920
And just run that off-policy algorithm

351
00:19:46,920 --> 00:19:55,740
but using the offline data set rather than recollecting data

352
00:19:55,740 --> 00:19:58,400
with our new policy

353
00:19:58,400 --> 00:19:59,780
Does anyone have thoughts on what

354
00:19:59,780 --> 00:20:03,580
will happen if you use an off policy algorithm

355
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:09,580
like soft actor critic on an offline data set

356
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:15,300
Yeah, so the thought there was that we have this--

357
00:20:15,300 --> 00:20:19,460
we're evaluating our Q-estimate on actions sampled

358
00:20:19,460 --> 00:20:21,490
from our policy, and our policy is also

359
00:20:21,490 --> 00:20:26,530
trying to increase likelihood on Q-values on states that have--

360
00:20:26,530 --> 00:20:28,530
or on actions that have high Q-values

361
00:20:28,530 --> 00:20:30,430
And if we have an offline data set

362
00:20:30,430 --> 00:20:31,850
and we're learning a policy that's

363
00:20:31,850 --> 00:20:36,250
different from the policy that collected the data, then

364
00:20:36,250 --> 00:20:38,990
this Q-estimate that we're supervising

365
00:20:38,990 --> 00:20:42,490
we're using to supervise our Q-function may not be accurate

366
00:20:42,490 --> 00:20:46,770
Because it didn't actually necessarily see

367
00:20:46,770 --> 00:20:52,450
actions in the data that come from the policy we're learning

368
00:20:52,450 --> 00:20:55,210
And so we might actually end up learning an accurate Q-function

369
00:20:55,210 --> 00:20:57,090
And if we don't learn an accurate Q-function

370
00:20:57,090 --> 00:21:01,410
then we might get a bad policy as a result

371
00:21:01,410 --> 00:21:03,930
And so to walk through that a little bit more

372
00:21:03,930 --> 00:21:09,360
let's say that we, maybe, again--

373
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:11,030
you can have a little bit of space here

374
00:21:11,030 --> 00:21:19,440
So let's say that we have our data set and some of the data

375
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:21,190
Here are a few of the actions in the data

376
00:21:21,190 --> 00:21:22,570
Maybe this is a1

377
00:21:22,570 --> 00:21:23,810
This is a2

378
00:21:23,810 --> 00:21:25,650
This is a3

379
00:21:25,650 --> 00:21:28,770
And there's other actions that the car could take

380
00:21:28,770 --> 00:21:30,190
that's not in the data set

381
00:21:30,190 --> 00:21:32,810
So for example, maybe the car could

382
00:21:32,810 --> 00:21:34,930
go on an adventure over here

383
00:21:34,930 --> 00:21:37,250
Maybe that's a4

384
00:21:37,250 --> 00:21:40,130
The car could also make a U-turn

385
00:21:40,130 --> 00:21:42,010
That's a5

386
00:21:42,010 --> 00:21:43,970
The car could also go backwards

387
00:21:43,970 --> 00:21:45,490
Maybe that's a6

388
00:21:45,490 --> 00:21:48,810
So there's all actions that the car could do

389
00:21:48,810 --> 00:21:51,570
And at the beginning of training

390
00:21:51,570 --> 00:21:56,770
we're going to be randomly initializing our Q-function

391
00:21:56,770 --> 00:22:02,490
And so if you think about some randomly initialized Q-function

392
00:22:02,490 --> 00:22:13,170
for a given state, maybe it looks somewhat random

393
00:22:13,170 --> 00:22:18,920
And this is for different actions

394
00:22:18,920 --> 00:22:23,320
Then only some of these actions are present in the data

395
00:22:23,320 --> 00:22:28,000
And so maybe a1, a2, and a3, maybe they're

396
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:33,760
all within this range

397
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:37,960
So this is what's like in distribution in the data

398
00:22:37,960 --> 00:22:40,760
And then outside of that, your Q-function

399
00:22:40,760 --> 00:22:43,120
is somewhat arbitrary

400
00:22:43,120 --> 00:22:46,880
And you don't actually see any data for outside that

401
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:48,940
And then when you do a policy update

402
00:22:48,940 --> 00:22:50,520
your policy is going to say, oh, maybe

403
00:22:50,520 --> 00:22:55,640
let's try to increase the likelihood on actions

404
00:22:55,640 --> 00:22:57,480
that have a high Q-value

405
00:22:57,480 --> 00:23:04,360
This is actions a, Q of s, a

406
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:06,020
Then what it will do is, it'll say, OK

407
00:23:06,020 --> 00:23:08,800
for this randomly initialized Q value, maybe something

408
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:11,160
like this looks good

409
00:23:11,160 --> 00:23:13,620
And then if that's outside your data

410
00:23:13,620 --> 00:23:16,850
then it will still continue to think

411
00:23:16,850 --> 00:23:18,720
that that's a good action because it'll never

412
00:23:18,720 --> 00:23:25,400
actually know what the proper Q-value for that action is

413
00:23:25,400 --> 00:23:29,760
And so walking through that, basically, everything--

414
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:32,940
Maybe another way to think about this is that here's your data

415
00:23:32,940 --> 00:23:38,480
Here are some of the actions that are out of distribution

416
00:23:38,480 --> 00:23:40,800
including a4, a5

417
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:45,220
It never saw a car that, for example

418
00:23:45,220 --> 00:23:47,440
makes a U-turn on the highway

419
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:50,960
And so the Q-value is randomly initialized for that

420
00:23:50,960 --> 00:23:53,040
Some of those actions that are out of distribution

421
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:55,820
will arbitrarily have higher key values

422
00:23:55,820 --> 00:23:58,320
Some will have lower because it's randomly initialized

423
00:23:58,320 --> 00:24:01,600
and then your policy will exploit those inaccuracies

424
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:06,120
on the out of distribution actions

425
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:12,200
So on the slides, we have this term right here

426
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:15,390
When you evaluate this Q-function on a prime

427
00:24:15,390 --> 00:24:17,390
that's not in the data set, if you

428
00:24:17,390 --> 00:24:21,710
have some randomly initialized Q-function for that state

429
00:24:21,710 --> 00:24:24,550
and your data support only covers part of that

430
00:24:24,550 --> 00:24:27,090
then on the out-of-distribution actions

431
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:29,230
your Q-function will be unreliable

432
00:24:29,230 --> 00:24:30,970
And then when you do your policy update

433
00:24:30,970 --> 00:24:33,630
that's going to seek out actions, where the Q-function is

434
00:24:33,630 --> 00:24:36,230
overoptimistic

435
00:24:36,230 --> 00:24:37,850
And then after the policy update

436
00:24:37,850 --> 00:24:41,130
these key values will be overestimated

437
00:24:41,130 --> 00:24:43,590
because now you're going to keep on reinforcing

438
00:24:43,590 --> 00:24:48,520
that was good behavior, even when it's not in your data

439
00:24:48,520 --> 00:24:50,190
Another perspective on this is that this

440
00:24:50,190 --> 00:24:53,350
will happen in scenarios where your learn policy is deviating

441
00:24:53,350 --> 00:24:55,500
too much from your behavior policy

442
00:24:55,500 --> 00:24:57,250
Because these out-of-distribution actions

443
00:24:57,250 --> 00:24:59,470
These are things that are very different from what the behavior

444
00:24:59,470 --> 00:25:00,370
policy did

445
00:25:00,370 --> 00:25:02,750
And if we stayed closer to the behavior policy

446
00:25:02,750 --> 00:25:06,290
then we wouldn't have this issue

447
00:25:06,290 --> 00:25:08,750
The question is, what happens if you have a discrete action

448
00:25:08,750 --> 00:25:09,510
space

449
00:25:09,510 --> 00:25:15,950
So basically, everything will be the same

450
00:25:15,950 --> 00:25:17,950
It's possible that your data set

451
00:25:17,950 --> 00:25:20,410
if in all the states in your data set

452
00:25:20,410 --> 00:25:22,730
it has full coverage of the actions

453
00:25:22,730 --> 00:25:25,870
then you won't have this problem because everything

454
00:25:25,870 --> 00:25:28,270
will be in distribution

455
00:25:28,270 --> 00:25:30,510
But in practice, usually, there will

456
00:25:30,510 --> 00:25:33,390
be states, where you don't have full coverage of all actions

457
00:25:33,390 --> 00:25:36,670
And you can still see overestimation in those states

458
00:25:36,670 --> 00:25:38,640
Yeah, so the question is, can you just exclude

459
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:40,310
out-of-distribution actions, when you're

460
00:25:40,310 --> 00:25:42,630
updating your Q-function

461
00:25:42,630 --> 00:25:45,350
Yeah, and there are approaches for doing that

462
00:25:45,350 --> 00:25:47,990
Now, the unfortunate thing is we don't actually

463
00:25:47,990 --> 00:25:50,650
know what's in distribution and out of distribution

464
00:25:50,650 --> 00:25:54,650
So we have just samples of states and actions

465
00:25:54,650 --> 00:25:57,390
And for a given state, you might have

466
00:25:57,390 --> 00:25:59,890
one state that then chooses a1

467
00:25:59,890 --> 00:26:01,270
Then you might have another state

468
00:26:01,270 --> 00:26:03,610
that's not exactly the same, but very similar

469
00:26:03,610 --> 00:26:04,770
And that takes a2

470
00:26:04,770 --> 00:26:10,460
And so in that case, a2 might technically not

471
00:26:10,460 --> 00:26:12,040
be seen for the first state

472
00:26:12,040 --> 00:26:13,540
But you don't necessarily know-- you

473
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:15,710
don't know for a given state, what's in distribution

474
00:26:15,710 --> 00:26:18,420
and what's out of distribution

475
00:26:18,420 --> 00:26:21,460
And yeah, we'll basically see--

476
00:26:21,460 --> 00:26:23,500
basically, a key idea behind offline RL methods

477
00:26:23,500 --> 00:26:25,400
is trying to avoid--

478
00:26:25,400 --> 00:26:27,020
well, first trying to think about how

479
00:26:27,020 --> 00:26:29,700
do we deviate not too much from the behavior policy

480
00:26:29,700 --> 00:26:34,380
And second, can we avoid out-of-distribution actions

481
00:26:34,380 --> 00:26:39,220
OK, so let's talk about mitigating overestimation

482
00:26:39,220 --> 00:26:44,180
This is really the core goal behind offline RL methods

483
00:26:44,180 --> 00:26:52,540
So the one approach to offline RL is basically

484
00:26:52,540 --> 00:26:53,880
to do imitation learning

485
00:26:53,880 --> 00:26:56,700
And the reason why imitation learning works

486
00:26:56,700 --> 00:26:59,380
is that you're only supervising with actions

487
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:00,860
that are seen in the data

488
00:27:00,860 --> 00:27:04,060
And so any approach that uses imitation learning

489
00:27:04,060 --> 00:27:07,780
is going to be avoiding out-of-distribution actions

490
00:27:07,780 --> 00:27:10,060
somewhat implicitly because you're only

491
00:27:10,060 --> 00:27:14,260
going to be supervising with actions that are in the data

492
00:27:14,260 --> 00:27:16,700
Now, we don't necessarily want to use vanilla imitation

493
00:27:16,700 --> 00:27:20,360
learning because the offline data may not be optimal

494
00:27:20,360 --> 00:27:22,280
There might be human drivers in your data set

495
00:27:22,280 --> 00:27:24,660
for example, that don't drive in the way

496
00:27:24,660 --> 00:27:28,980
that you want your autonomous vehicle to drive

497
00:27:28,980 --> 00:27:32,900
And offline RL methods should provide a way

498
00:27:32,900 --> 00:27:36,180
to be better than the policy that underlies

499
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:39,860
the data by leveraging reward information

500
00:27:39,860 --> 00:27:41,820
by leveraging the fact that maybe you

501
00:27:41,820 --> 00:27:44,260
can go through your data set and say that this

502
00:27:44,260 --> 00:27:47,100
was a behavior that was good

503
00:27:47,100 --> 00:27:52,160
And maybe this was a behavior that was not as good

504
00:27:52,160 --> 00:27:53,700
And that reward information can allow

505
00:27:53,700 --> 00:27:57,150
you to get a better policy

506
00:27:57,150 --> 00:27:58,900
And then, yeah, vanilla imitation learning

507
00:27:58,900 --> 00:28:03,610
can't outperform the expert or what's provided in the data

508
00:28:03,610 --> 00:28:04,980
The other thing that I'll mention

509
00:28:04,980 --> 00:28:10,090
is that I'm not sure if there's an analogous situation

510
00:28:10,090 --> 00:28:12,570
Maybe there is actually probably some analogous situation

511
00:28:12,570 --> 00:28:15,410
in driving, but a good offline RL method can also

512
00:28:15,410 --> 00:28:17,770
stitch together good behaviors

513
00:28:17,770 --> 00:28:23,650
So if you have-- where to put this

514
00:28:23,650 --> 00:28:29,370
Say you have-- maybe let's put it over here

515
00:28:29,370 --> 00:28:32,250
So say you have some trajectory in your offline data set

516
00:28:32,250 --> 00:28:34,130
that looks something like this

517
00:28:34,130 --> 00:28:39,450
And another trajectory of your data set

518
00:28:39,450 --> 00:28:43,050
maybe looks something like this, and say

519
00:28:43,050 --> 00:28:45,850
that this one got a good reward at the end

520
00:28:45,850 --> 00:28:49,610
and this one got a negative reward at the end

521
00:28:49,610 --> 00:28:52,010
Then a good offline RL method should realize

522
00:28:52,010 --> 00:28:55,910
that this is a good behavior

523
00:28:55,910 --> 00:28:58,490
And also, the first part of this trajectory

524
00:28:58,490 --> 00:29:03,370
if you continue along the trajectory at the end also

525
00:29:03,370 --> 00:29:05,910
can be a good behavior and a good offer

526
00:29:05,910 --> 00:29:10,750
RL method should realize that when you're in these states

527
00:29:10,750 --> 00:29:15,250
you should essentially be able to stitch together

528
00:29:15,250 --> 00:29:16,730
the first part of this trajectory

529
00:29:16,730 --> 00:29:19,730
and the second part of the green trajectory

530
00:29:19,730 --> 00:29:22,170
And if you use vanilla imitation learning

531
00:29:22,170 --> 00:29:23,990
you won't get that behavior

532
00:29:23,990 --> 00:29:26,550
Instead, what it will do is, in this state

533
00:29:26,550 --> 00:29:33,370
it will take the average between these two action distributions

534
00:29:33,370 --> 00:29:36,210
So what this could look like is, if you have a couple

535
00:29:36,210 --> 00:29:40,560
trajectories like this, then we know that S1 to S3

536
00:29:40,560 --> 00:29:41,270
is good behavior

537
00:29:41,270 --> 00:29:44,330
We know S7 to S9 is good behavior

538
00:29:44,330 --> 00:29:47,170
And so offline reinforcement learning methods, in principle

539
00:29:47,170 --> 00:29:52,970
can actually learn how to get a policy that goes from S1 to S9

540
00:29:52,970 --> 00:29:55,290
whereas, imitation learning methods won't

541
00:29:55,290 --> 00:29:57,770
be able to do that

542
00:29:57,770 --> 00:30:04,680
So before we get into methods that can do that stitching

543
00:30:04,680 --> 00:30:09,760
let's first talk about a very simple baseline method

544
00:30:09,760 --> 00:30:16,960
So one idea is if you have a offline data set

545
00:30:16,960 --> 00:30:19,880
you could also just take the trajectories

546
00:30:19,880 --> 00:30:22,200
that have high rewards and only imitate

547
00:30:22,200 --> 00:30:26,760
the stuff that is good and don't imitate the stuff that's bad

548
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:29,720
And that seems like a reasonable method

549
00:30:29,720 --> 00:30:33,720
You have some offline data set that contains

550
00:30:33,720 --> 00:30:36,560
a variety of trajectories

551
00:30:36,560 --> 00:30:39,160
Then, what you could do is, you could

552
00:30:39,160 --> 00:30:43,520
define a new data set that has only trajectories that

553
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:46,000
have a high reward

554
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,840
And so what this new data set would look like is

555
00:30:48,840 --> 00:30:51,000
it would be all of the trajectories

556
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:54,960
in your original data set, except conditioned

557
00:30:54,960 --> 00:30:59,160
on the reward of that trajectory, being greater

558
00:30:59,160 --> 00:31:01,580
than some threshold

559
00:31:01,580 --> 00:31:05,720
And that threshold could be some percentile of the rewards

560
00:31:05,720 --> 00:31:08,880
in your data set, like the 70th percentile, for example

561
00:31:08,880 --> 00:31:12,440
only taking the top 30% of trajectories

562
00:31:12,440 --> 00:31:15,400
And then from there, you could learn

563
00:31:15,400 --> 00:31:21,680
a policy that maximizes the likelihood of the actions

564
00:31:21,680 --> 00:31:24,160
in this modified data set

565
00:31:24,160 --> 00:31:28,200
So that would look like taking the states and actions

566
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:35,040
in this modified data set and maximizing the log probability

567
00:31:35,040 --> 00:31:37,540
of the actions given the state

568
00:31:37,540 --> 00:31:42,280
So this is the typical imitation learning objective

569
00:31:42,280 --> 00:31:43,680
Cool

570
00:31:43,680 --> 00:31:47,760
So this is, perhaps, our first offline RL method

571
00:31:47,760 --> 00:31:50,560
I guess, very, very basic

572
00:31:50,560 --> 00:31:53,040
Filter the data set to do the top k percent of data

573
00:31:53,040 --> 00:31:55,520
and then imitate the filtered data set

574
00:31:55,520 --> 00:31:56,920
This is a very primitive approach

575
00:31:56,920 --> 00:31:58,910
for using reward information

576
00:31:58,910 --> 00:32:01,270
And it's worth noting that it's not

577
00:32:01,270 --> 00:32:02,890
able to do this stitching behavior

578
00:32:02,890 --> 00:32:05,690
Because what it will do is, if this is a bad trajectory

579
00:32:05,690 --> 00:32:08,110
it might just filter out that trajectory entirely

580
00:32:08,110 --> 00:32:10,670
and it won't actually learn how to take

581
00:32:10,670 --> 00:32:12,630
this good behavior and this good behavior

582
00:32:12,630 --> 00:32:17,190
and figure out how to get from here to here

583
00:32:17,190 --> 00:32:18,970
That said, it's a good baseline

584
00:32:18,970 --> 00:32:21,930
So if you're going to be trying out any offline RL method

585
00:32:21,930 --> 00:32:25,990
it's good to try this first and see how well it does

586
00:32:25,990 --> 00:32:27,850
and use that as a benchmark

587
00:32:27,850 --> 00:32:29,910
And make sure that-- or ideally, try

588
00:32:29,910 --> 00:32:32,470
to make sure that your offline RL method is doing better

589
00:32:32,470 --> 00:32:34,390
than this

590
00:32:34,390 --> 00:32:37,670
OK, now, one of the reasons why I introduced

591
00:32:37,670 --> 00:32:41,290
this is that, arguably, some of the best offline RL methods

592
00:32:41,290 --> 00:32:42,750
are actually doing something that's

593
00:32:42,750 --> 00:32:45,270
not all that different from this, in that they do

594
00:32:45,270 --> 00:32:48,870
have imitation learning component to them

595
00:32:48,870 --> 00:32:54,830
And the idea here is that instead of just taking

596
00:32:54,830 --> 00:32:59,070
the good trajectories, we could imitate the data

597
00:32:59,070 --> 00:33:01,070
but weight our imitation learning

598
00:33:01,070 --> 00:33:09,630
objective based on how good the action was in that transition

599
00:33:09,630 --> 00:33:13,990
Now, if you remember what we talked about with regard

600
00:33:13,990 --> 00:33:16,250
to value functions and advantage functions

601
00:33:16,250 --> 00:33:19,710
where the advantage function is how much better is a t, compared

602
00:33:19,710 --> 00:33:24,550
to what your policy does, this advantage function

603
00:33:24,550 --> 00:33:29,430
suggests that if we weighted our imitation learning objective

604
00:33:29,430 --> 00:33:31,350
by the advantage function, then we

605
00:33:31,350 --> 00:33:33,390
could get some measure of how good that action is

606
00:33:33,390 --> 00:33:38,230
and get a policy that is able to learn effectively

607
00:33:38,230 --> 00:33:44,170
And this will form the objective of our first offline RL method

608
00:33:44,170 --> 00:33:46,790
And in particular, what we'll do is

609
00:33:46,790 --> 00:33:54,430
we will maximize an objective for a policy that

610
00:33:54,430 --> 00:33:58,580
will be taking the standard imitation learning

611
00:33:58,580 --> 00:34:10,460
objective, but just weighting it by the exponentiated advantages

612
00:34:10,460 --> 00:34:15,300
And this will be a sum over states and actions in our data

613
00:34:15,300 --> 00:34:17,900
set

614
00:34:17,900 --> 00:34:21,440
In terms of the choice to exponentiate them

615
00:34:21,440 --> 00:34:26,699
there's actually a way to derive this particular objective

616
00:34:26,699 --> 00:34:28,460
And I'll mention that on the slides

617
00:34:28,460 --> 00:34:30,000
We won't go through the derivation

618
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:32,020
But if you're interested, we can see if we

619
00:34:32,020 --> 00:34:34,900
can post it after lecture

620
00:34:34,900 --> 00:34:40,580
And in terms of this objective, if we have a good advantage

621
00:34:40,580 --> 00:34:44,820
function, and we go back to the stitching example

622
00:34:44,820 --> 00:34:48,659
for this particular state, right here

623
00:34:48,659 --> 00:34:56,969
we know that the advantage of this action is high, right

624
00:34:56,969 --> 00:35:01,730
Because the value, the Q-value of this is 1

625
00:35:01,730 --> 00:35:06,250
The Q-value for going this way, at least in the data, is 0

626
00:35:06,250 --> 00:35:11,010
and the value of this state will be the average of those two

627
00:35:11,010 --> 00:35:12,250
outcomes

628
00:35:12,250 --> 00:35:14,470
And so the advantage for this one will be positive

629
00:35:14,470 --> 00:35:19,370
And the advantage for going this direction will be negative

630
00:35:19,370 --> 00:35:22,810
And so when we then take our imitation learning objective

631
00:35:22,810 --> 00:35:25,090
and weight it by the advantages, we're

632
00:35:25,090 --> 00:35:27,650
going to be telling our policy in that state

633
00:35:27,650 --> 00:35:30,010
it should go to the good outcome rather than

634
00:35:30,010 --> 00:35:32,130
going to the bad outcome

635
00:35:32,130 --> 00:35:35,350
And so this objective, if you have a good advantage estimate

636
00:35:35,350 --> 00:35:38,570
is capable of stitching together good behaviors

637
00:35:38,570 --> 00:35:40,830
in an offline data set

638
00:35:40,830 --> 00:35:42,250
And so in terms of the derivation

639
00:35:42,250 --> 00:35:45,610
you can show that this advantage weighted imitation learning

640
00:35:45,610 --> 00:35:51,370
objective is approximating a KL constraint objective

641
00:35:51,370 --> 00:35:58,010
Where you can show that it's maximizing the future

642
00:35:58,010 --> 00:36:01,250
rewards, as measured by your key value, subject to the constraint

643
00:36:01,250 --> 00:36:05,250
that you're staying close to your behavior policy

644
00:36:05,250 --> 00:36:10,090
And this is a pretty reasonable objective for offline RL

645
00:36:10,090 --> 00:36:12,650
Because if you're too far from your behavior policy

646
00:36:12,650 --> 00:36:17,130
then you won't be able to--

647
00:36:17,130 --> 00:36:21,250
you won't be able to actually get good information about how

648
00:36:21,250 --> 00:36:27,090
to improve your policy

649
00:36:27,090 --> 00:36:31,290
So before we talk about how we estimate the advantage

650
00:36:31,290 --> 00:36:34,330
and what this advantage will be, any questions

651
00:36:34,330 --> 00:36:36,770
on what this objective looks like

652
00:36:36,770 --> 00:36:38,570
Yeah, so the question is for this

653
00:36:38,570 --> 00:36:40,270
to improve over imitation learning

654
00:36:40,270 --> 00:36:43,010
do we need to assume that our trajectories have

655
00:36:43,010 --> 00:36:44,490
intersecting states

656
00:36:44,490 --> 00:36:49,980
And the brief answer is that, no, we generally

657
00:36:49,980 --> 00:36:51,060
don't have to assume that

658
00:36:51,060 --> 00:36:52,840
And in this diagram, I actually tried

659
00:36:52,840 --> 00:36:57,080
to have S7 and S3 not be exactly the same state

660
00:36:57,080 --> 00:36:59,880
And the reason why we don't need them to be exactly the same

661
00:36:59,880 --> 00:37:03,360
is that if we're using a neural network to represent

662
00:37:03,360 --> 00:37:06,920
the policy and the advantage function

663
00:37:06,920 --> 00:37:09,630
then it will naturally--

664
00:37:09,630 --> 00:37:11,920
States and actions that are very similar to each other

665
00:37:11,920 --> 00:37:14,320
will naturally have similar outputs

666
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:15,740
from that neural network

667
00:37:15,740 --> 00:37:18,760
And so it will be able to--

668
00:37:18,760 --> 00:37:21,640
it should be able to generalize, if the states

669
00:37:21,640 --> 00:37:24,360
are similar to each other, but not exactly intersecting

670
00:37:24,360 --> 00:37:29,000
Now, the degree to this how well this works will depend on

671
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:32,520
how close those states are and how large the data set

672
00:37:32,520 --> 00:37:34,520
is, such that it can learn a representation that

673
00:37:34,520 --> 00:37:37,680
is collapsing those representations to something

674
00:37:37,680 --> 00:37:39,420
that looks similar

675
00:37:39,420 --> 00:37:40,880
The question is, in practice, do we

676
00:37:40,880 --> 00:37:44,400
need to worry about the exponential term exploding

677
00:37:44,400 --> 00:37:47,080
When we look at a practical implementation of this

678
00:37:47,080 --> 00:37:50,920
we will actually add a hyperparameter that corresponds

679
00:37:50,920 --> 00:37:53,160
to the temperature of this

680
00:37:53,160 --> 00:38:01,840
And so the practical algorithm will have 1 over alpha times

681
00:38:01,840 --> 00:38:03,340
the advantage

682
00:38:03,340 --> 00:38:10,100
And that can help prevent this from getting extremely large

683
00:38:10,100 --> 00:38:10,600
Yeah

684
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:16,400
And then, I guess it depends a bit--

685
00:38:16,400 --> 00:38:18,320
maybe we'll talk about it also a bit more when

686
00:38:18,320 --> 00:38:20,880
we get into fitting advantages

687
00:38:20,880 --> 00:38:23,020
Yeah, I knew this question would come up

688
00:38:23,020 --> 00:38:25,880
So when we talked about actor critic methods

689
00:38:25,880 --> 00:38:28,360
the objective looked similar to this

690
00:38:28,360 --> 00:38:29,740
except instead of exponentiating

691
00:38:29,740 --> 00:38:31,400
we just had the advantage here

692
00:38:31,400 --> 00:38:33,560
The other key difference was that here, we're

693
00:38:33,560 --> 00:38:36,260
sampling from our offline data set

694
00:38:36,260 --> 00:38:40,320
And in the actor-critic objectives

695
00:38:40,320 --> 00:38:42,880
the action, well, there's different versions

696
00:38:42,880 --> 00:38:45,000
but we're sampling from an online data set

697
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:49,350
data set, typically, and in soft actor-critic

698
00:38:49,350 --> 00:38:51,450
this action actually comes from your policy

699
00:38:51,450 --> 00:38:54,910
not from the data set

700
00:38:54,910 --> 00:39:00,990
There isn't a very quick way to derive where

701
00:39:00,990 --> 00:39:04,470
those differences come from

702
00:39:04,470 --> 00:39:08,250
One, I don't want to confuse you

703
00:39:08,250 --> 00:39:12,390
but the one way of thinking about this

704
00:39:12,390 --> 00:39:16,990
is, oftentimes, online RL objectives will be optimizing

705
00:39:16,990 --> 00:39:19,590
a reverse scale objective, and offline are looking more

706
00:39:19,590 --> 00:39:21,050
at a forward tail objective

707
00:39:21,050 --> 00:39:25,010
But it's a longer explanation

708
00:39:25,010 --> 00:39:26,310
Yeah

709
00:39:26,310 --> 00:39:28,150
OK

710
00:39:28,150 --> 00:39:32,310
And there's also some references here as well

711
00:39:32,310 --> 00:39:34,910
So we've talked about this advantage

712
00:39:34,910 --> 00:39:39,270
Now, advantage actually have some policy

713
00:39:39,270 --> 00:39:42,110
associated with them

714
00:39:42,110 --> 00:39:46,310
It's the advantage of taking action a in state S

715
00:39:46,310 --> 00:39:48,590
and then following some policy

716
00:39:48,590 --> 00:39:50,310
And so the question is--

717
00:39:50,310 --> 00:39:53,310
the first question is, which policy are we trying

718
00:39:53,310 --> 00:39:56,190
to estimate the advantage for

719
00:39:56,190 --> 00:39:59,990
For now, we can try to estimate the advantage of the behavior

720
00:39:59,990 --> 00:40:02,830
policy

721
00:40:02,830 --> 00:40:04,590
And then a key question comes up is

722
00:40:04,590 --> 00:40:08,430
how do we want to estimate this advantage function, ideally

723
00:40:08,430 --> 00:40:10,470
in a way that prevents the overestimation that we

724
00:40:10,470 --> 00:40:14,310
saw before

725
00:40:14,310 --> 00:40:19,790
So the first way that we can estimate the advantage

726
00:40:19,790 --> 00:40:23,270
function-- I mean, generally, it will look very

727
00:40:23,270 --> 00:40:26,790
similar to what we saw before, when estimating the advantage

728
00:40:26,790 --> 00:40:27,290
function

729
00:40:27,290 --> 00:40:29,830
But there's a couple other considerations

730
00:40:29,830 --> 00:40:33,510
So let's say version--

731
00:40:33,510 --> 00:40:36,210
maybe I'll do this in a new color

732
00:40:36,210 --> 00:40:44,260
So let's say version 1, it's v1

733
00:40:44,260 --> 00:40:47,380
Version 1, we're going to use the simplest

734
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:51,100
thing that we had before, which is to use Monte Carlo returns

735
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:56,140
So we can first fit a value function

736
00:40:56,140 --> 00:40:59,100
And because we're fitting the value function for the behavior

737
00:40:59,100 --> 00:41:03,540
policy, we don't need to--

738
00:41:03,540 --> 00:41:04,340
it is coming--

739
00:41:04,340 --> 00:41:05,760
The data is coming from the policy

740
00:41:05,760 --> 00:41:08,620
that we're trying to evaluate a value function for

741
00:41:08,620 --> 00:41:14,420
So if we want to fit a value function with Monte Carlo

742
00:41:14,420 --> 00:41:18,540
returns, that our objective looks--

743
00:41:18,540 --> 00:41:26,980
we're trying to regress to the sum of future rewards

744
00:41:26,980 --> 00:41:33,340
So our objective looks something like this

745
00:41:33,340 --> 00:41:35,300
So this is something that basically supervised

746
00:41:35,300 --> 00:41:38,180
learning to the future rewards

747
00:41:38,180 --> 00:41:42,440
And then, once we do this, which is fairly straightforward

748
00:41:42,440 --> 00:41:46,900
then the question is, how do we get the advantage out of this

749
00:41:46,900 --> 00:41:49,220
There's a couple ways you could do this

750
00:41:49,220 --> 00:41:53,160
In the first time that we saw this

751
00:41:53,160 --> 00:41:58,220
the way that we got the advantage function

752
00:41:58,220 --> 00:42:08,660
for a given state and action was to use the reward plus the value

753
00:42:08,660 --> 00:42:13,580
function at the next time step minus the value function

754
00:42:13,580 --> 00:42:15,080
at the current time step

755
00:42:15,080 --> 00:42:21,540
And these are all the value function that we just estimated

756
00:42:21,540 --> 00:42:25,260
So you could use this

757
00:42:25,260 --> 00:42:29,900
The one alternative-- so you could use this

758
00:42:29,900 --> 00:42:32,660
This is going to rely on having a value function that

759
00:42:32,660 --> 00:42:36,820
is fairly accurate, because you're doing this one step

760
00:42:36,820 --> 00:42:39,810
temporal difference between the reward

761
00:42:39,810 --> 00:42:43,130
plus the value here, minus the value at the current state

762
00:42:43,130 --> 00:42:45,050
And so one alternative thing that you could do

763
00:42:45,050 --> 00:42:52,050
is, instead of using this estimated--

764
00:42:52,050 --> 00:42:56,730
this reward plus value, which is basically the estimated

765
00:42:56,730 --> 00:42:59,890
future rewards, if you start at state t

766
00:42:59,890 --> 00:43:05,990
and take action a minus your current value is

767
00:43:05,990 --> 00:43:12,010
instead, you could simply use the empirical sum of rewards

768
00:43:12,010 --> 00:43:14,050
into the future

769
00:43:14,050 --> 00:43:30,530
And so this could simply be the--

770
00:43:30,530 --> 00:43:33,210
and this is going to be a little bit simpler

771
00:43:33,210 --> 00:43:38,770
and doesn't rely on having a good temporal difference

772
00:43:38,770 --> 00:43:41,130
between the two

773
00:43:41,130 --> 00:43:43,490
And so, specifically, we can first

774
00:43:43,490 --> 00:43:45,880
estimate the value function by trying

775
00:43:45,880 --> 00:43:48,090
to minimize the difference between our value estimate

776
00:43:48,090 --> 00:43:49,970
and the sum of rewards

777
00:43:49,970 --> 00:43:53,550
And then to estimate the advantage function

778
00:43:53,550 --> 00:43:59,050
we can take the sum of rewards for that trajectory

779
00:43:59,050 --> 00:44:02,330
and subtract the value

780
00:44:02,330 --> 00:44:07,290
In terms of this particular advantage

781
00:44:07,290 --> 00:44:11,930
estimate corresponds to a fairly simple and fairly popular

782
00:44:11,930 --> 00:44:18,610
algorithm called advantage weighted regression

783
00:44:18,610 --> 00:44:24,690
And I should emphasize that the way that we did it, previously

784
00:44:24,690 --> 00:44:29,090
where you used r plus V of s t plus 1 is also valid

785
00:44:29,090 --> 00:44:30,730
This simply corresponds to a method

786
00:44:30,730 --> 00:44:33,330
that is used pretty commonly, and seems

787
00:44:33,330 --> 00:44:37,440
to work well in practice, when you have an offline data set

788
00:44:37,440 --> 00:44:39,800
The question was we talked about before

789
00:44:39,800 --> 00:44:42,840
how this is somewhat high variance, and GAE is better

790
00:44:42,840 --> 00:44:46,080
You could also use GAE here, definitely

791
00:44:46,080 --> 00:44:49,320
This is going to be a little bit simpler

792
00:44:49,320 --> 00:44:53,720
But yeah, you could also use generalized advantage estimation

793
00:44:53,720 --> 00:44:54,530
here

794
00:44:54,530 --> 00:44:55,740
Yeah, that's a good question

795
00:44:55,740 --> 00:44:59,340
So the question is, are we bringing up this Monte Carlo

796
00:44:59,340 --> 00:45:01,800
because, with offline data set, we

797
00:45:01,800 --> 00:45:04,380
have enough data that sum of rewards is more accurate

798
00:45:04,380 --> 00:45:07,240
and we don't have the variance issues that we saw before

799
00:45:07,240 --> 00:45:08,600
That's definitely one reason

800
00:45:08,600 --> 00:45:10,520
When you have a large offline data set

