00:59:53,850 --> 00:59:55,560
and upweighting it when it's less than 0

1062
00:59:55,560 --> 01:00:05,380
And the reverse will be true, when lambda is greater than 0.5

1063
01:00:05,380 --> 01:00:07,000
So what this looks like is instead of--

1064
01:00:07,000 --> 01:00:08,860
I guess I drew it slightly wrong there

1065
01:00:08,860 --> 01:00:11,070
Instead of using a loss function, it looks like this

1066
01:00:11,070 --> 01:00:12,760
You can use an asymmetric loss function

1067
01:00:12,760 --> 01:00:20,000
that is penalizing errors less in one direction

1068
01:00:20,000 --> 01:00:22,240
This is called expected regression

1069
01:00:22,240 --> 01:00:24,000
It's a cool trick

1070
01:00:24,000 --> 01:00:27,400
You can think about it in terms of rather

1071
01:00:27,400 --> 01:00:32,120
than just error, some arbitrary random variable x

1072
01:00:32,120 --> 01:00:36,920
And if you define different values of this lambda term

1073
01:00:36,920 --> 01:00:39,720
then you get these asymmetric losses

1074
01:00:39,720 --> 01:00:43,640
that look like this, where, again, if lambda

1075
01:00:43,640 --> 01:00:46,310
is greater than 0.5, then you see

1076
01:00:46,310 --> 01:00:48,640
it as asymmetric in this direction versus, if it's less

1077
01:00:48,640 --> 01:00:54,080
you see asymmetric in the other direction

1078
01:00:54,080 --> 01:00:55,560
And here's another visualization

1079
01:00:55,560 --> 01:00:57,880
where if you're trying to--

1080
01:00:57,880 --> 01:01:01,980
the mean of this random variable is shown in the red curve

1081
01:01:01,980 --> 01:01:04,120
but then, you can get different tiles

1082
01:01:04,120 --> 01:01:09,000
with different values of lambda

1083
01:01:09,000 --> 01:01:12,640
So then coming back to our algorithms

1084
01:01:12,640 --> 01:01:17,480
if we want to fit our value function

1085
01:01:17,480 --> 01:01:22,800
we can use this expected loss and basically

1086
01:01:22,800 --> 01:01:25,680
fit a value function that is trying

1087
01:01:25,680 --> 01:01:27,640
to estimate a value function that's

1088
01:01:27,640 --> 01:01:29,360
better than-- for a policy, that's

1089
01:01:29,360 --> 01:01:31,000
better than a behavior policy

1090
01:01:31,000 --> 01:01:33,800
And this will be penalizing you for overestimating

1091
01:01:33,800 --> 01:01:35,520
and underestimating, but it won't

1092
01:01:35,520 --> 01:01:39,390
be penalizing you as much, if you overestimate a little bit

1093
01:01:39,390 --> 01:01:41,100
Basically, if you're off by a little bit

1094
01:01:41,100 --> 01:01:44,320
And this means that when you have these different outcomes

1095
01:01:44,320 --> 01:01:49,780
and these different outcomes right here, if you get the mean

1096
01:01:49,780 --> 01:01:50,840
you get 3

1097
01:01:50,840 --> 01:01:54,160
Whereas, if you have--

1098
01:01:54,160 --> 01:01:57,740
I haven't written down exactly what the optimum would be

1099
01:01:57,740 --> 01:02:05,230
But for some value of lambda, it'll be optimal instead

1100
01:02:05,230 --> 01:02:07,230
Instead of outputting the mean, it'll

1101
01:02:07,230 --> 01:02:09,110
be more optimal to output something

1102
01:02:09,110 --> 01:02:11,990
like 6, for example, where you're

1103
01:02:11,990 --> 01:02:17,670
penalized less for overestimating this value

1104
01:02:17,670 --> 01:02:19,240
And yeah, the outcome of the loss

1105
01:02:19,240 --> 01:02:20,990
function when you use some things together

1106
01:02:20,990 --> 01:02:28,010
will be something that's higher than the mean

1107
01:02:28,010 --> 01:02:29,550
So in particular, for this algorithm

1108
01:02:29,550 --> 01:02:31,930
we'll use the expected loss to fit the value function

1109
01:02:31,930 --> 01:02:34,470
We'll actually also fit a Q-function

1110
01:02:34,470 --> 01:02:37,670
with the typical mean squared error loss

1111
01:02:37,670 --> 01:02:42,790
And we'll actually use the value function as the target

1112
01:02:42,790 --> 01:02:46,430
So we'll be fitting both the value function and a Q-function

1113
01:02:46,430 --> 01:02:48,750
And we'll just be using this expected loss

1114
01:02:48,750 --> 01:02:51,030
for fitting the value function

1115
01:02:51,030 --> 01:02:55,230
And then once we have our Q-function and value function

1116
01:02:55,230 --> 01:02:58,550
then we can estimate the advantages Q minus V

1117
01:02:58,550 --> 01:03:03,550
and use the same AWR method to get a policy, where

1118
01:03:03,550 --> 01:03:05,270
we're weighting on the likelihoods

1119
01:03:05,270 --> 01:03:10,610
by the exponentiated Q minus V

1120
01:03:10,610 --> 01:03:11,990
Yeah, so the question is, will we

1121
01:03:11,990 --> 01:03:14,370
be overestimating the value in this case

1122
01:03:14,370 --> 01:03:17,030
So one thing to note about this algorithm

1123
01:03:17,030 --> 01:03:20,990
is, we're never going to be passing as input actions that

1124
01:03:20,990 --> 01:03:22,590
are not in the data

1125
01:03:22,590 --> 01:03:25,070
And that was one of the major sources of overestimation

1126
01:03:25,070 --> 01:03:26,270
before

1127
01:03:26,270 --> 01:03:28,410
Now depending on how you set lambda

1128
01:03:28,410 --> 01:03:34,390
if you set lambda to be 0, then you will overestimate probably

1129
01:03:34,390 --> 01:03:37,230
And typically, you'll choose some value

1130
01:03:37,230 --> 01:03:40,590
that's maybe 0.2 or 0.2, something

1131
01:03:40,590 --> 01:03:44,550
like that's more in the middle that's not so aggressive

1132
01:03:44,550 --> 01:03:50,380
And you will be getting an overestimate of the pi beta

1133
01:03:50,380 --> 01:03:51,750
And the reason for that is you're

1134
01:03:51,750 --> 01:03:54,080
actually trying to estimate the value of a policy that's

1135
01:03:54,080 --> 01:03:55,230
better than pi beta

1136
01:03:55,230 --> 01:03:59,780
So you're trying to estimate

1137
01:03:59,780 --> 01:04:07,740
V pi for a policy pi that's better than pi beta

1138
01:04:07,740 --> 01:04:13,380
So you're purposefully overestimating the pi beta

1139
01:04:13,380 --> 01:04:16,060
The question is, because we're going to be gradually improving

1140
01:04:16,060 --> 01:04:20,220
our policy, would it make sense to gradually decrease lambda

1141
01:04:20,220 --> 01:04:23,360
So we're not actually collecting data from our policy

1142
01:04:23,360 --> 01:04:27,740
And so all the data is coming from our offline data set

1143
01:04:27,740 --> 01:04:31,260
And in that case, it makes sense to keep it constant

1144
01:04:31,260 --> 01:04:33,380
because you always want to be a fixed amount better

1145
01:04:33,380 --> 01:04:34,900
than your behavior policy

1146
01:04:34,900 --> 01:04:36,660
Now, if you did in principle actually

1147
01:04:36,660 --> 01:04:39,520
do start collecting some online data

1148
01:04:39,520 --> 01:04:43,180
then things will change a little bit, because then you'll

1149
01:04:43,180 --> 01:04:47,400
actually be skewing your data set to a better policy

1150
01:04:47,400 --> 01:04:49,900
And in that case, it actually might make sense, potentially

1151
01:04:49,900 --> 01:04:54,700
to bring lambda closer to 0, or sorry, closer to 0.5

1152
01:04:54,700 --> 01:04:57,300
Because you'll then have data from a better policy--

1153
01:04:57,300 --> 01:05:00,740
a policy better than your behavior policy

1154
01:05:00,740 --> 01:05:02,420
So pros of this algorithm, we don't

1155
01:05:02,420 --> 01:05:04,440
need to query out of distribution actions

1156
01:05:04,440 --> 01:05:06,980
The policy is still only trained on actions in the data

1157
01:05:06,980 --> 01:05:09,880
And we're decoupling actor and critic training

1158
01:05:09,880 --> 01:05:12,200
So we just do these critic training

1159
01:05:12,200 --> 01:05:15,200
We're fitting a value function and a Q-function on our data

1160
01:05:15,200 --> 01:05:16,660
And then once we're done with that

1161
01:05:16,660 --> 01:05:19,980
and that's converged, or we've done enough training

1162
01:05:19,980 --> 01:05:24,860
then we train our policy

1163
01:05:24,860 --> 01:05:25,500
Cool

1164
01:05:25,500 --> 01:05:28,160
So this is an algorithm called implicit Q-learning

1165
01:05:28,160 --> 01:05:30,160
Yeah, so the question is where are we fitting Q

1166
01:05:30,160 --> 01:05:33,460
So we could also use Monte Carlo returns with this

1167
01:05:33,460 --> 01:05:39,100
and use the spectral-like objective with this term

1168
01:05:39,100 --> 01:05:44,380
It's nice to use Q because Q is lower variance than this sum

1169
01:05:44,380 --> 01:05:46,460
of rewards right here

1170
01:05:46,460 --> 01:05:53,370
And we can fit a Q-function, using the bootstrapped backups

1171
01:05:53,370 --> 01:05:55,450
So all these ideas are applicable to Monte Carlo

1172
01:05:55,450 --> 01:05:56,810
as well

1173
01:05:56,810 --> 01:05:59,050
But using Q-functions, like when you

1174
01:05:59,050 --> 01:06:02,050
can use this bootstrapped objective, which you could only

1175
01:06:02,050 --> 01:06:06,250
do with Q-functions, then you'll get a lower variance target

1176
01:06:06,250 --> 01:06:08,990
And then I guess to summarize the point of offline RL

1177
01:06:08,990 --> 01:06:11,090
So online data is really expensive

1178
01:06:11,090 --> 01:06:14,170
because we have to recollect it every time we run our algorithm

1179
01:06:14,170 --> 01:06:18,170
And it's really, really useful to reuse offline data

1180
01:06:18,170 --> 01:06:19,890
One of the big challenges with offline RL

1181
01:06:19,890 --> 01:06:21,810
is this shift between your behavior

1182
01:06:21,810 --> 01:06:23,730
policy and your learn policy

1183
01:06:23,730 --> 01:06:27,410
And we talked about a filtered or weighted imitation

1184
01:06:27,410 --> 01:06:29,410
learning method as a baseline

1185
01:06:29,410 --> 01:06:32,330
And we talked about how we can actually explicitly constrain

1186
01:06:32,330 --> 01:06:34,930
the policy to the behavior policy

1187
01:06:34,930 --> 01:06:38,850
by only supervising the policy on actions in your data

1188
01:06:38,850 --> 01:06:41,810
And so that allowed us to get rid or move away

1189
01:06:41,810 --> 01:06:45,410
from this distribution shift problem

1190
01:06:45,410 --> 01:06:47,450
And then the trajectory stitching idea

1191
01:06:47,450 --> 01:06:49,770
that we talked about before allows us to improve over

1192
01:06:49,770 --> 01:06:53,610
vanilla imitation learning

1193
01:06:53,610 --> 01:06:57,130
This example application is for the algorithm we didn't cover

1194
01:06:57,130 --> 01:06:59,370
If you want to train offline filter

1195
01:06:59,370 --> 01:07:01,450
behavior cloning and implicit Q-learning

1196
01:07:01,450 --> 01:07:04,490
are both good approaches, but implicit Q-learning

1197
01:07:04,490 --> 01:07:07,970
is going to be probably work better, if you have

1198
01:07:07,970 --> 01:07:10,460
especially if you have a broader data set

1199
01:07:10,460 --> 01:07:12,210
And if you want to do offline pre-training

1200
01:07:12,210 --> 01:07:16,090
and online fine-tuning, IQL is actually a very good algorithm

1201
01:07:16,090 --> 01:07:16,790
for doing that

1202
01:07:16,790 --> 01:07:20,090
It can also be used with online data collection

1203
01:07:20,090 --> 01:07:22,730
quite effectively, and it works quite well there

1204
01:07:22,730 --> 01:07:24,850
There's also a variant of IQL called

1205
01:07:24,850 --> 01:07:29,850
IDQL that uses diffusion policies for your policy class

1206
01:07:29,850 --> 01:07:31,410
right here

1207
01:07:31,410 --> 01:07:33,620
And it also works quite well

1208
01:07:33,620 --> 01:07:35,370
And so if you're interested in diving more

1209
01:07:35,370 --> 01:07:37,410
into IQL-like algorithms, I would encourage

1210
01:07:37,410 --> 01:07:39,250
you to check out IDQL

1211
01:07:39,250 --> 01:07:41,470
It is actually-- beyond using diffusion

1212
01:07:41,470 --> 01:07:43,010
it also is a bit different in terms

1213
01:07:43,010 --> 01:07:45,480
of how this update is done

